{
  "comment": "ADR 0205 decisions 4, 6 and 8: what a sandbox boot makes of one CURIE_ATTACHMENTS_REF payload. Two substrates redeem the same payload and must agree case by case: the rendered attachments-init program (executed by charts/curie/ci/attachment-init-behavior-assertions.sh against a fixture HTTP server) and the docker driver's _prepare_attachments (apps/worker/tests/sandbox/test_docker_attachment_claim.py, OpenerDirector.open faked). Each entry is the wire entry (n/u/s/b/e/m) plus 'c': 1 for a file on the current message, 0 for an earlier one. A payload whose entries carry NO 'c' is an older worker's (mixed-version rollout) and keeps the pre-ADR behavior for the whole payload: every entry is current and all-or-nothing, the name is cleaned to its basename (blank, '.', '..' refused) and a duplicate is disambiguated with a numeric suffix before the extension (report.pdf, report-2.pdf, ...), skipping a name already taken. Exact-name strictness (unclean, reserved '.curie-' or duplicate name fatal; whitespace-only fatal) applies only to a payload that carries 'c'. 'serve' says how the store answers: ok serves the body; expired mints the reference with a closed window (never fetched); http_404/http_403/http_500 answer with that status; redirect answers 302 to a location that would serve the body (a redirect is refused, never followed); stall sends the headers and one byte and then goes silent past the fetch deadline; drip sends one byte at a time, each well inside a socket timeout, so the whole body takes longer than the fetch deadline; oversize serves cap_bytes + 1 bytes whose digest is the minted one (only the per-file cap can refuse it); digest_mismatch serves bytes other than the ones the digest was minted from. outcome 'fatal' means the boot is refused and nothing is visible in the mount; refused_before_fetch additionally means no entry of the payload was requested. 'ok' means 'visible' is exactly the set of non-hidden files in the mount (name -> bytes) and, when 'status' is not null, the status file holds exactly 'status' (order-insensitive by name; a reason given as a list accepts any of its members). With 'status' null (a legacy payload) the status file may or may not be written. Every harness also checks, for every case: no current entry is fetched after an earlier one, an expired entry is never fetched, an entry recorded 'deadline' that is not itself a drip is never fetched, nothing is written outside the mount, and a drip is abandoned at the deadline rather than read to the end.",
